2.  THE DEGENERACY.  (M_b, a_0) -> (lambda M_b, a_0/lambda) must leave the profile unchanged if (C1) holds.
    Here is by how much it actually changes across the radii KiDS measures.
----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
   log M_b  lambda    0.035Mpc     0.100Mpc     0.300Mpc     1.000Mpc     2.600Mpc
       9.0    0.25      -1.64%       -0.58%       -0.19%       -0.06%       -0.02%
       9.0    4.00       6.74%        2.34%        0.78%        0.23%        0.09%
      10.0    0.25      -5.05%       -1.82%       -0.61%       -0.18%       -0.07%
      10.0    4.00      21.77%        7.47%        2.47%        0.74%        0.28%
      11.0    0.25     -14.61%       -5.56%       -1.91%       -0.58%       -0.22%
      11.0    4.00      71.51%       24.17%        7.87%        2.34%        0.90%

  RETRACTION OF MY OWN FIRST DRAFT: I wrote that the degeneracy is exact everywhere KiDS measures. It is not.
  It is exact in the deep branch (2.5% at R > 0.1 Mpc for lenses below 1e10 Msun) and it BREAKS
  at the inner edge of the most massive bin (72%), because there R_inner/r_M is only 2.9.
  [PASS] K07c.3 the corrected, two-sided statement: the (M_b, a_0) degeneracy is EXACT in the deep branch and BREAKS only where the data reach inside the transition radius. Both halves are asserted, so the check fails if either the deep branch is not degenerate or the inner edge is   (compensating factor-4 rescale changes Delta Sigma by 2.5% at R > 0.1 Mpc for M_b <= 1e10 (degenerate) but by 72% at R = 35 kpc for M_b = 1e11 (not degenerate); my blanket 'a few per cent everywhere' is withdrawn)

